<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=optical-telegraph">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Optical telegraph</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1792">1792</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as semaphore telegraph</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An optical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> is a line of stations, typically towers, that pass text along by visual signals; credit for the first successful one goes to the French engineer Claude Chappe and his brothers in 1792, and Chappe's word télégraphe gave English its 'telegraph'. Operators watched the neighbouring tower through a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telescope">telescope</a> and passed on the positions of its semaphore arms; the Chappe network came to cover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> with 556 stations over 4,800 kilometres. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> built the second network, and about half a century later the electrical telegraph replaced such lines.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/optical-telegraph.jpg" alt="Optical telegraph" width="320" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zubro · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chappe_Telegraph.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Optical telegraphy is ancient, in the form of torches, smoke signals and hydraulic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraphs</a>. In 1684 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-hooke">Robert Hooke</a> outlined a system of visual telegraphy to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> that was never put into practice. Sir Richard Lovell Edgeworth experimented with optical signalling in 1767, when he bet Lord March that he could send news of a race result in one hour. The Chappe brothers began work in 1790, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> needed swift military communications while surrounded by hostile forces. Claude Chappe considered sound, smoke and even electricity before settling on an optical system and a first public demonstration between Brûlon and Parcé, 16 kilometres apart.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>There are two main types: the semaphore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a>, whose pivoted arms convey information by the direction they point, and the shutter telegraph, whose rotating panels pass or block the light from the sky behind. Relay towers stood within sight of each other at separations of 5 to 20 miles, 8 to 32 kilometres. Operators watched the neighbouring tower through a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telescope">telescope</a> and passed on each message they saw. Chappe coined the word semaphore in 1801 from Greek elements meaning sign and carrying. The system was faster than post riders over long distances and cheaper to run once built, but its range was limited by geography and weather, and it could not cross wide water unless an island served as a relay station.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Other countries imitated the Chappe system, especially after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> used it to coordinate his empire and army. Abraham Niclas Edelcrantz built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>'s three-station experimental line in 1794, using ten iron shutters that gave 1,024 code points; the central station was at the Katarina Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>, and the last stationary semaphore link in regular service, in Sweden, closed in 1880. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> the Admiralty accepted Lord George Murray's shutter system in September 1795 and its first chain ran from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to Deal; in 1816 Sir Home Popham's semaphore replaced it.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> built lines, and the electrical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a>, cheaper, faster and more private, later replaced them all. The networks have been called the first data networks, with control characters, routing and error control.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Optical telegraph” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1792</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>France</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Claude Chappe</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Robert Hooke outlined a visual telegraph in 1684, and the Chappe brothers first demonstrated theirs in 1791.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/telescope">Telescope<span>1608</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=optical-telegraph">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
